<section #l1-corollary | `<corollary>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · Layer 1 `<corollary>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix for the corollary's label, parallel to <theorem>'s `name` kwarg. |
| `+numbered` | boolean | default `true` | Whether this corollary participates in the propositional theorem-family shared counter. Default true. |

Produced by: canonical `<corollary>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-corollary-1>
<corollary | Every prime greater than 2 is odd.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-corollary-1>
<corollary | Every prime greater than 2 is odd.>
</minipage>

<code #code:l1-corollary-2>
<corollary #cor:bounded>
Any continuous function on a closed interval is bounded.
</corollary>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-corollary-2>
<corollary #cor:bounded>
Any continuous function on a closed interval is bounded.
</corollary>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #l1-definition | `<definition>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · Layer 1 `<definition>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix for the definition's label. |
| `+numbered` | boolean | default `true` | Whether this definition participates in the definition counter. <definition> runs on its own counter (separate from the propositional theorem-family counter), matching amsthm's conventional "definition" theorem-style family. Default true. |

Produced by: canonical `<definition>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-definition-1>
<definition | A *group* is a set with an associative binary operation, identity, and inverses.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-definition-1>
<definition | A *group* is a set with an associative binary operation, identity, and inverses.>
</minipage>

<code #code:l1-definition-2>
<definition name="Group" #def:group>
A *group* is a set $G$ together with a binary operation
$\cdot$ satisfying associativity, identity, and inverses.
</definition>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-definition-2>
<definition name="Group" #def:group>
A *group* is a set $G$ together with a binary operation
$\cdot$ satisfying associativity, identity, and inverses.
</definition>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #l1-example | `<example>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · Layer 1 `<example>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix for the example's label. |
| `+numbered` | boolean | default `true` | Whether this example participates in the example counter. <example> runs on its own counter (separate from theorem, lemma, definition counters), matching amsthm's conventional "example" theorem-style family. Default true. |

Produced by: canonical `<example>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-example-1>
<example | The integers under addition form a group.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-example-1>
<example | The integers under addition form a group.>
</minipage>

<code #code:l1-example-2>
<example #ex:integers>
The integers $\mathbb{Z}$ under addition form a group: the
operation is associative, $0$ is the identity, and every
integer has an additive inverse.
</example>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-example-2>
<example #ex:integers>
The integers $\mathbb{Z}$ under addition form a group: the
operation is associative, $0$ is the identity, and every
integer has an additive inverse.
</example>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #l1-lemma | `<lemma>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · Layer 1 `<lemma>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix for the lemma's label, parallel to <theorem>'s `name` kwarg. Honored by the Phase-2 handler. |
| `+numbered` | boolean | default `true` | Whether this lemma participates in the propositional theorem- family shared counter. Default true; -numbered suppresses. |

Produced by: canonical `<lemma>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-lemma-1>
<lemma | Every continuous function on a compact set attains its maximum.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-lemma-1>
<lemma | Every continuous function on a compact set attains its maximum.>
</minipage>

<code #code:l1-lemma-2>
<lemma name="Zorn" #lem:zorn>
Every non-empty partially-ordered set in which every chain has
an upper bound contains a maximal element.
</lemma>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-lemma-2>
<lemma name="Zorn" #lem:zorn>
Every non-empty partially-ordered set in which every chain has
an upper bound contains a maximal element.
</lemma>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #l1-proof | `<proof>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · Layer 1 `<proof>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix — e.g. "Proof (of Theorem 1.2)" or "Proof (sketch)". Honored by the Phase-2 handler. |
| `+numbered` | boolean | default `false` | Whether this proof is numbered. Default false — proofs are conventionally unnumbered (a proof's identity comes from the theorem it proves, not from a counter). An author who wants numbering can opt-in per instance with +numbered. |

Produced by: canonical `<proof>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-proof-1>
<theorem | The sum of two even integers is even.>

<proof>
Let $a = 2m$ and $b = 2n$. Then $a + b = 2(m + n)$, which is
even. $\square$
</proof>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-proof-1>
<theorem | The sum of two even integers is even.>

<proof>
Let $a = 2m$ and $b = 2n$. Then $a + b = 2(m + n)$, which is
even. $\square$
</proof>
</minipage>

*The canonical pattern: &lt;theorem&gt; followed by sibling &lt;proof&gt;. The proof is NOT nested inside the theorem.*

<code #code:l1-proof-2>
<proof name="of Theorem 1.2">
The argument follows from the preceding lemma.
</proof>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-proof-2>
<proof name="of Theorem 1.2">
The argument follows from the preceding lemma.
</proof>
</minipage>

*Optional `name` kwarg lets the proof identify what it proves (useful when the proof is separated from its theorem by intervening text).*

</section>

<section #l1-proposition | `<proposition>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · Layer 1 `<proposition>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix for the proposition's label. |
| `+numbered` | boolean | default `true` | Whether this proposition participates in the propositional theorem-family shared counter. Default true. |

Produced by: canonical `<proposition>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-proposition-1>
<proposition | The sum of two even integers is even.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-proposition-1>
<proposition | The sum of two even integers is even.>
</minipage>

<code #code:l1-proposition-2>
<proposition name="Cauchy-Schwarz">
For any vectors $u$, $v$ in an inner-product space,
$|\langle u, v \rangle| \le \|u\| \, \|v\|$.
</proposition>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-proposition-2>
<proposition name="Cauchy-Schwarz">
For any vectors $u$, $v$ in an inner-product space,
$|\langle u, v \rangle| \le \|u\| \, \|v\|$.
</proposition>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #l1-remark | `<remark>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · Layer 1 `<remark>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix for the remark's label. Honored by the Phase-2 handler even though <remark> is unnumbered (a named remark may render as "Remark (Name):" without a number). |
| `+numbered` | boolean | default `false` | Whether this remark is numbered. Default false — <remark> is conventionally unnumbered (the "remark" theorem-style family in amsthm is unnumbered). An author who wants numbering can opt-in per instance with +numbered, but most usage relies on the default. |

Produced by: canonical `<remark>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-remark-1>
<remark | The converse does not hold in general.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-remark-1>
<remark | The converse does not hold in general.>
</minipage>

<code #code:l1-remark-2>
<remark>
The hypothesis of compactness is essential here; without it
the conclusion fails (consider $f(x) = 1/x$ on $(0, 1]$).
</remark>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-remark-2>
<remark>
The hypothesis of compactness is essential here; without it
the conclusion fails (consider $f(x) = 1/x$ on $(0, 1]$).
</remark>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #l1-theorem | `<theorem>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `theorem-family` · Layer 1 `<theorem>` · JATS `<statement>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | Optional name suffix for the theorem's label (the "(Pythagoras)" half of "Theorem 1.2 (Pythagoras)"). Honored by the Phase-2 theorem handler at label-rendering time; until that handler lands, the kwarg flows through to the rendered HTML as `data-name="..."` via schema dispatch. LaTeX amsthm precedent: `\begin{theorem}[Pythagoras]`. |
| `+numbered` | boolean | default `true` | Whether this theorem participates in the theorem-family shared counter (see "Numbering" below). Use +numbered (default) to number; -numbered to suppress. Can also be written as numbered=true / numbered=false. |

Produced by: canonical `<theorem>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-theorem-1>
<theorem | If $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$ then the triangle is right-angled.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-theorem-1>
<theorem | If $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$ then the triangle is right-angled.>
</minipage>

*Short-form with pipe content. The body is parsed normally.*

<code #code:l1-theorem-2>
<theorem name="Pythagoras" #thm:pyth>
If $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$, the triangle with sides $a$, $b$, $c$
is right-angled.
</theorem>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-theorem-2>
<theorem name="Pythagoras" #thm:pyth>
If $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$, the triangle with sides $a$, $b$, $c$
is right-angled.
</theorem>
</minipage>

*Long-form with the optional name kwarg. Cross-referenceable via id (the "thm:" colon-prefix convention is consistent with "fig:", "eqn:", "sec:" elsewhere in enscribe). The name kwarg lifts to `data-name`; the Phase-2 handler will render it as the "(Pythagoras)" suffix to the label "Theorem N".*

</section>

